**Cause of the flake:** the test calls `new Date()` twice, once for the input and once for the expected value. If midnight passes between the calls, the two dates fall on different days and the assertion fails. The expected value is also computed with `addDays`, which makes the test partly circular.

**Fix:** `dueDate` takes the issue date as an argument, so the test doesn't need the real clock. It uses fixed dates and literal expected results.

```js
// src/billing/due.test.js
const { dueDate } = require('./due');

describe('dueDate', () => {
  // Fixed dates built with the local-time constructor (year, monthIndex, day),
  // so results don't depend on when the test runs.
  const cases = [
    ['mid-month',               new Date(2024, 0, 15),  new Date(2024, 1, 14)],
    ['crosses a month end',     new Date(2024, 0, 31),  new Date(2024, 2, 1)],   // leap year: Feb has 29 days
    ['non-leap February',       new Date(2023, 0, 31),  new Date(2023, 2, 2)],
    ['crosses a year end',      new Date(2024, 11, 15), new Date(2025, 0, 14)],
    ['spans a DST change (US)', new Date(2024, 2, 1),   new Date(2024, 2, 31)],
  ];

  test.each(cases)('invoice issued %s is due 30 days later', (_name, issued, expected) => {
    const result = dueDate(issued);
    expect(result.getFullYear()).toBe(expected.getFullYear());
    expect(result.getMonth()).toBe(expected.getMonth());
    expect(result.getDate()).toBe(expected.getDate());
  });

  test('keeps the time of day of the issue date', () => {
    const issued = new Date(2024, 0, 15, 23, 59, 59);
    const result = dueDate(issued);
    expect(result.getDate()).toBe(14);
    expect(result.getHours()).toBe(23);
    expect(result.getMinutes()).toBe(59);
  });

  test('does not mutate the input date', () => {
    const issued = new Date(2024, 0, 15);
    const before = issued.getTime();
    dueDate(issued);
    expect(issued.getTime()).toBe(before);
  });
});
```

The tests compare year, month and day instead of raw timestamps. That way a DST shift in the host timezone can't cause a one-hour mismatch. If `due.js` works in UTC (`setUTCDate`, `Date.UTC`), I'd switch the constructors to `new Date(Date.UTC(...))` and the getters to `getUTC*`.

